NNAAMMEE
     mmaacc__sseeeeootthheerruuiiddss — simple policy controlling whether users see other
     users

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile the policy into your kernel, place the following lines in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ooppttiioonnss MMAACC
           ooppttiioonnss MMAACC__SSEEEEOOTTHHEERRUUIIDDSS

     Alternately, to load the module at boot time, place the following line in
     your kernel configuration file:

           ooppttiioonnss MMAACC

     and in loader.conf(5):

           mac_seeotheruids_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmaacc__sseeeeootthheerruuiiddss policy module, when enabled, denies users to see
     processes or sockets owned by other users.

     To enable mmaacc__sseeeeootthheerruuiiddss, set the sysctl OID
     _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._m_a_c_._s_e_e_o_t_h_e_r_u_i_d_s_._e_n_a_b_l_e_d to 1.  To permit superuser awareness of
     other credentials by virtue of privilege, set the sysctl OID
     _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._m_a_c_._s_e_e_o_t_h_e_r_u_i_d_s_._s_u_s_e_r___p_r_i_v_i_l_e_g_e_d to 1.

     To allow users to see processes and sockets owned by the same primary
     group, set the sysctl OID _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._m_a_c_._s_e_e_o_t_h_e_r_u_i_d_s_._p_r_i_m_a_r_y_g_r_o_u_p___e_n_a_b_l_e_d
     to 1.

     To allow processes with a specific group ID to be exempt from the policy,
     set the sysctl OID _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._m_a_c_._s_e_e_o_t_h_e_r_u_i_d_s_._s_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_g_i_d___e_n_a_b_l_e_d to 1,
     and _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._m_a_c_._s_e_e_o_t_h_e_r_u_i_d_s_._s_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_g_i_d to the group ID to be exempted.

